              <h3>Abstract</h3>
              <p>This project digitally documents Ghora Gali, an archaeological
                    site in the Himalayan mountains of Indian Occupied Kashmir
                    featuring around 200 stone sculptures. Despite its historical
                    and cultural significance, Ghora Gali remains largely unrecorded
                    and at risk of erasure. Using photography, videography, GPS
                    geotagging, and 3D modeling, I will create an online archive
                    that will preserve Ghora Gali's material history and local
                    folklore, making it accessible to scholars and the public.</p>
                <h3>Intellectual Ambitions and Objectives</h3>
               <p>Growing up, I was told never to go to Ghora Gali. Ghora Gali
                    (horse alley) Gool in Indian Occupied Kashmir, is considered off
                    limits, shrouded in local cautionary tales and folklore. My
                    mother, like many others in my village who couldn't read or write
                    but carried vast archives in their memories, would lower her voice
                    whenever the name Ghora Gali came up.</p>
               <p>Folklore goes that in the Himalayan forests, long, long ago, a
                    wedding procession climbed the mountain pass: men, women and
                    children on horses, drums echoing across the Pir Panjal range.
                    Before they could reach their destination and the marriage could
                    be solemnized, the jinns (demons/spirits) arrived and overpowered
                    the wedding party, turning everyone into stone. The wedding froze
                    in time, the jinn never left. Haunted by this sinister reputation,
                    Ghora Gali is considered a cursed site where those who dare to
                    visit risk being turned into stone themselves. For decades, this
                    warning kept me away.</p>
               <p>Only later did I learn that Ghora Gali is one of the most
                    significant archaeological sites in India. It is located in the
                    armed conflict-ridden Gool area of Ramban district in the Indian
                    occupied Jammu and Kashmir with around two hundred unique stone
                    sculptures of mounted horsemen. They are variously referred to as
                    the "Horsemen of the Pir Panjal," "Kashmir's Lost Hun Riders,"
                    or simply, the "Petrified Wedding".</p>
                <p>The artistic style of the sculptures is widely believed to
                    reflect Bactrian, Indo-Greek, or post-Hellenistic influences. It
                    is completely unknown who carved these sculptures and when. Some
                    local news reports date the sculptures to between the 7th and
                    10th centuries and link them to trans-Himalayan movements,
                    Central Asian riders, or frontier militarization along ancient
                    routes. What is certain is that the site has never been fully
                    excavated. It lies exposed both to the harsh Himalayan climate
                    and to the political violence that has rendered much of Jammu
                    and Kashmir inaccessible, militarized, and epistemically erased.</p>
               <p>Ghora Gali is in an abandoned state of neglect due to its long
                    colonial history and armed conflict, its location in a "most
                    backward" and inaccessible terrain, and, more recently,
                    administrative apathy that fails to protect the decaying stone
                    sculptures from weather and theft. Despite its historical
                    importance, archaeological density, and transregional significance,
                    Ghora Gali remains digitally unpreserved and institutionally
                    neglected, with no recognition from UNESCO. The stone sculptures
                    at Ghora Gali have been damaged over time -- some are headless
                    and many weathered into near-erasure.</p>
                <p>My project develops a critical digital humanities investigation
                    centered on Ghora Gali's enigmatic and undocumented stone
                    sculptures. It currently exists only in scattered social media
                    photographs, oral accounts, and non-systematic narrative
                    documentation. There is no preserved archive, 3D visualizations,
                    or metadata standardization. This absence not only limits
                    comparative art-historical analysis but risks complete erasure.
                    In an armed conflict and the most militarized zone on earth
                    creating a digital archive of this neglected historically and
                    archaeologically important site is crucial.</p>
               <p>The goal of my project is multifold: to digitally preserve
                    Ghora Gali and the folktales associated with it; to make this
                    digital archive available online for other scholars worldwide
                    (for free) and spark their interest in archaeological excavation
                    and preservation of Ghora Gali; and to make a strong foundation
                    for petitioning the Archaeological Survey of India and UNESCO to
                    take immediate action to preserve Ghora Gali and declare it a
                    World Heritage Site.</p>
                <p>The central intellectual ambition of this project is to
                    transform Ghora Gali from a precarious and informal heritage site
                    into a methodologically explicit, digitally preserved, and reusable
                    scholarly resource, while critically interrogating what it means
                    to preserve heritage in a politically sensitive and bureaucratically
                    constrained region. What happens when we place archaeological
                    stones alongside a mother's admonition? When do we read sculptures
                    not only as objects of the past, but as witnesses to interrupted
                    futures? At Ghora Gali, history did not end. It hardened and
                    waited.</p>
                <h3>Methodology</h3>
               <p>Beyond my mother's warning and local news reports endures a
                    deeper truth: Ghora Gali exists simultaneously as heritage and
                    haunt. It is a place where stone holds memory, where folklore
                    functions as an archive, and where fear is not superstition but
                    historical knowledge produced under conditions of occupation and
                    abandonment. This project begins from that tension. Rather than
                    treating local narratives as myths to be corrected by science,
                    I approach them as theory -- as ethical frameworks through which
                    communities have long negotiated danger, sovereignty, and loss.</p>
               <p>The petrified wedding folklore is not simply about spirits; it
                    is about why certain spaces are left untouched, why knowledge is
                    withheld, and why some histories survive only in whispered
                    prohibitions. In a region where militarization has restricted
                    movement and scholarship alike, folklore has become a tool of
                    historical preservation. My primary motivation behind this project
                    is to document and keep alive the rich culture of these folktales
                    for future generations.</p>
                <p>My project moves beyond narrative travelogues and social media
                    posts. I employ a hybrid digital humanities methodology combining
                    digital archaeology, static web publishing, structured data design,
                    and critical interpretive scholarship.</p>
               <p>a. Digital archaeology, 3D modeling and geotagging using GPS:
                    I plan to photograph, videograph, and geotag all 200 stone
                    sculptures at Ghora Gali during a three-week visit in March 2026.
                    My ultimate ambition is to use high-definition 2D photographs to
                    create 3D models of each sculpture type using SAM 3D or Copilot
                    3D -- both free and open. These models will be available in open,
                    interoperable formats enabling reuse across platforms and long-term
                    preservation.</p>
               <p>b. Static digital publishing and embedded interactivity: I will
                    publish my project as a static website built with Jekyll and hosted
                    on GitHub Pages. Static websites are lightweight, stable, and
                    sustainable. The website will have pages divided into categories
                    of sculptures, with each page displaying 2D photographs and a 3D
                    model embedded using Google Model Viewer, which supports open
                    access without proprietary lock-in.</p>
                <p>c. Structured metadata and reusability: Each documented sculpture
                    will be accompanied by structured metadata, including a unique
                    identifier, material and condition notes, iconographic description,
                    stylistic and comparative notes (e.g., Central Asian or Indo-Greek
                    parallels), and sculpture category. This transforms the project
                    from a descriptive website into a reusable scholarly resource.</p>
                <p>d. Critical paper: Alongside technical documentation, the project
                    will lead to a scholarly paper addressing the politics of
                    archaeological neglect in Kashmir, bureaucratic regimes of
                    preservation and abandonment, and digital heritage as
                    counter-infrastructure. Digital preservation becomes an ethical
                    and scholarly intervention, producing counter-infrastructures
                    resisting disappearance by stabilizing endangered material culture
                    in accessible form.</p>
